# Ground Truth — This Codebase's Real Constraints

Anchor every section below to how this app *actually* runs, not generic Laravel-at-scale assumptions. A review that spends its Performance section worrying about Horizon queue depth or Redis eviction policy is worthless here — those don't exist in this stack. Get these facts wrong and the rest of the review is noise.

- **Hosting**: Hostinger **shared hosting**, not a VPS/container. No root, no process supervisor, no persistent background processes.
- **Deploy**: a GitHub webhook hits a password-protected `/deployer` route, which runs `git fetch` + `git reset --hard origin/main` + Laravel cache-clears over PHP-FPM. `composer install` and `php artisan migrate` are **off by default** (`DEPLOYER_RUN_COMPOSER`/`DEPLOYER_RUN_MIGRATIONS`) because shared-hosting PHP requests have a short execution timeout — both have to be run manually over SSH after most feature deploys.
- **Queue**: `QUEUE_CONNECTION=sync`. There is no queue worker. Nothing marked `ShouldQueue` is actually deferred — it runs inline, in-request, synchronously, no matter what the code implies.
- **Broadcasting**: Pusher Channels (hosted, free Sandbox tier: 200k messages/day, 100 concurrent connections), not self-hosted Reverb — chosen specifically because this host can't run a persistent WebSocket process. Broadcast events here use `ShouldBroadcastNow`, not `ShouldBroadcast`, for the same reason: there's no queue to eventually deliver a queued one.
- **Cache/session**: file-based (`CACHE_STORE=file`, file sessions). No Redis, no Memcached in active use.
- **Database**: MySQL. No read replicas, no sharding, no query cache layer beyond what MySQL itself does.
- **Auth**: session-based (`auth`/`admin` middleware), not API tokens/Sanctum. A single `users` table serves both clients and admin/team members, distinguished by `role`/`is_super_admin`/`restricted_access` — there is no separate admin model or guard.
- **Admin permissions**: a custom, hand-maintained map (`App\Support\AdminPermissions::SECTIONS`) of route-name-wildcard patterns to restrictable sections, enforced by `EnsureUserCanAccessAdminPage` middleware. **A new admin route that isn't added to this map is silently unrestricted** — the middleware only enforces sections it recognizes.
- **Frontend**: server-rendered Blade, no SPA framework, no Vite build step running in production (Tailwind loads via CDN `<script>`; any new client-side dependency must be either vanilla-JS-CDN-loadable or the deploy pipeline needs rework). All existing JS is inline `<script>` per view — no bundler, no modules.
- **Payments**: Stripe, webhook-driven subscription/one-time-payment state.
- **Live data**: this system holds **real, paying client Stripe subscriptions today** — it is not a staging environment. Any destructive operation, migration touching existing rows, or billing-adjacent change carries real financial/legal weight, not just a bug-tracker ticket.

If the spec being reviewed assumes any capability this stack doesn't have (a queue worker, Redis, a persistent process, an SPA data layer, Sanctum tokens), **that is itself a Critical finding**, not a detail to gloss over.

