## 4. Performance Engineer

Review, given there is **no queue worker and no Redis** in this stack:
- Any synchronous `Mail::send()`, `broadcast()`, or outbound HTTP/API call sitting directly in the request path instead of behind the established `dispatch(fn () => ...)->afterResponse()` pattern (see Known Failure Patterns above) — this is the single most likely performance/reliability bug class here
- Whether this feature's expected message/event volume is anywhere near Pusher's free-tier caps (200k messages/day, 100 concurrent connections) — flag if realistic usage could approach either
- Caching — file-based cache only; is anything assumed to be fast-and-in-memory that will actually hit disk?
- Database load — see Database Engineer section; performance and DB review overlap heavily in this app given there's no caching layer to hide a slow query behind

Questions:
- Can this feature handle this app's actual realistic concurrency (a handful to a few dozen simultaneous users, not thousands) without a request blocking on a slow external call?
- What in this spec, if it becomes slow, has no fallback — i.e., a single blocking call with no timeout, no retry limit, no async escape hatch?

